# Indian Physiography: The Six Faces of the Subcontinent

> India's landscape divides into six macro-physiographic divisions —
> the mighty Himalayan wall, the world's most fertile river plains,
> the ancient peninsular plateau, the coastal strips, the deserts,
> and the islands. Each is a distinct story of tectonics, rivers, and
> time.

## 1. The Six Divisions

@diagram tree id="india-physiography" layout="tree" aria="Six physiographic divisions of India"
  "भारत का भू-आकृति विज्ञान / Indian Physiography" -> "Himalayan Mountains"
  "भारत का भू-आकृति विज्ञान / Indian Physiography" -> "Northern Plains"
  "भारत का भू-आकृति विज्ञान / Indian Physiography" -> "Peninsular Plateau"
  "भारत का भू-आकृति विज्ञान / Indian Physiography" -> "Indian Desert"
  "भारत का भू-आकृति विज्ञान / Indian Physiography" -> "Coastal Plains"
  "भारत का भू-आकृति विज्ञान / Indian Physiography" -> "Islands"
@/diagram

| Division | Area share | Key features |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Himalayas | ~10% | Highest, youngest fold mountains |
| Northern Plains | ~13% | Alluvial, extremely fertile |
| Peninsular Plateau | ~43% | Oldest landmass, rich minerals |
| Indian Desert | ~10% (of west) | Arid, sandy Thar |
| Coastal Plains | ~13% | Narrow western, broader eastern |
| Islands | ~0.2% | Andaman & Nicobar; Lakshadweep |

## 2. The Himalayas: Three Ranges, Three Widths

Formed by the Tethys Sea closure as the Indian plate collided with
Eurasia (~50 million years ago and still pushing ~5 cm/year), the
Himalayas run ~2,400 km west–east in parallel ranges:

| Range | Average height | Character |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Himadri (Greater Himalayas)** | 6,000 m | Continuous core; glaciers; Everest (8,849 m), Kanchenjunga |
| **Himachal (Lesser Himalayas)** | 3,700–4,500 m | Hill stations (Shimla, Mussoorie, Nainital, Darjeeling); duns (valleys like Dehradun) |
| **Shiwaliks (Outer)** | 900–1,100 m | Discontinuous, youngest; duns between ranges |

- **Regional divisions:** Punjab (Kashmir) Himalayas → Kumaon →
  Nepal → Assam Himalayas (west to east). The **Purvanchal** hills
  continue south of the Brahmaputra (Patkai, Naga, Manipur, Mizo —
  the Meghalaya plateau incl. Shillong stands apart).
- **Syntaxial bends:** the ranges sharply pivot at Nanga Parbat
  (west) and Namcha Barwa (east).
- **Passes:** Zoji La, Banihal, Nathu La (with China), Shipki La,
  Bomdila.

[info title="Why the Himalayas matter"]
They block the cold Central Asian winds, force the monsoon's ascent
(orographic rain), feed three great river systems (Indus, Ganga,
Brahmaputra) with glacier melt, and historically shielded the
subcontinent from northern invasions — geography shaping climate,
agriculture, and history at once.
[/info]

## 3. The Northern Plains: The Granary's Floor

Lying between the Himalayas and the peninsula, built of
Indo-Gangetic alluvium — thousands of metres deep — deposited over
millions of years:

| Division | Formation feature | Region |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Bhabar** | Pebble-strewn porous belt at Himalayan foot; streams vanish | 8–16 km wide |
| **Terai** | Streams re-emerge; marshy, malaria-prone, now largely reclaimed | South of Bhabar |
| **Bhangar** | Older alluvium, kankar (lime) nodules | Above flood level |
| **Khadar** | New alluvium, renewed by floods every year | The active floodplain |

About 700 km wide near the Ganga's mid-course, the plain holds the
densest rural population on Earth and grows most of India's wheat,
rice, and sugarcane.

## 4. The Peninsular Plateau: The Old, Stable Heart

[definition]
The **Peninsular Plateau** — India's oldest and most stable
landmass, part of the ancient Gondwana — is a tableland of
crystalline igneous and metamorphic rocks, rising 600–900 m,
tilted east, drained largely to the Bay of Bengal.
[/definition]

Two blocks divided by the **Narmada rift**:

- **Central Highlands** (north of Narmada): Malwa plateau,
  Chotanagpur plateau (Damodar trough; Jharkhand's coal and
  minerals), the Meghalaya and Aravalli outliers — the Aravallis
  (Guru Shikhar, 1,722 m) are among the world's oldest fold ranges,
  now relict and relict-eroded.
- **Deccan Plateau** (south): basaltic lava flows (the Deccan
  Traps), bounded by the **Western Ghats (Sahyadri)** — higher,
  continuous, Anai Mudi 2,695 m — and the **Eastern Ghats** — lower,
  dissected by rivers (Mahendragiri 1,501 m). The Nilgiri, Annamalai,
  and Cardamom hills join the two ranges at the plateau's southern
  tip.

[tip]
The Western Ghats force the south-west monsoon to rise, drenching
the windward Konkan–Malabar coast while the plateau's leeward side
sits in a **rain shadow** — one reason Mumbai gets ~2,200 mm while
Pune, barely 150 km inland, gets ~700 mm.
[/tip]

## 5. The Indian Desert: The Thar

West of the Aravallis lies the **Thar** — sandy, rolling dunes
(bhers), seasonal streams (Luni the only notable river), scrub
vegetation, and rainfall under 150 mm/yr. The Aravallis block
moisture-bearing winds; irrigation (Indira Gandhi Canal) now
greens its northern edge.

## 6. The Coastal Plains and the Islands

- **Western Coastal Plain:** narrow (10–50 km), between Ghats and
  Arabian Sea — Kachchh and Kathiawar (Gujarat), Konkan
  (Maharashtra), Kanara (Karnataka), Malabar (Kerala); dotted with
  lagoons (Vembanad, backwaters) and a series of ports, natural
  harbours included.
- **Eastern Coastal Plain:** wide (100–130 km), flat, deltaic —
  Utkal (Odisha), Coromandel (TN–AP); the Krishna–Godavari–Kaveri
  deltas make it the rice bowl; cyclone-prone in autumn.

| Islands | Origin | Character |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Andaman & Nicobar** | Mountainous; tectonic uplift, volcanic (Barren Island — India's only active volcano) | Equatorial rainforest, coral fringes |
| **Lakshadweep** | **Coral atolls** on the Laccadive-Chagos ridge | ~36 islands, coconut groves, lagoons |

## 7. Reading the Landscape Like a Geographer

Ask of any Indian landscape three questions: (1) *What built it?*
— plate tectonics, rivers, lava, or coral; (2) *What is wearing it
down?* — monsoon rivers, glaciers, or arid winds; (3) *What does it
offer people?* — fertile alluvium, mineral belts, ports, or
forests. Every division answers differently, and together they
explain India's population map, its crops, and its strategic
frontiers.

[summary]
India's six physiographic divisions: the young, three-ranged
Himalayas (Himadri–Himachal–Shiwaliks) blocking cold winds and
shaping the monsoon; the deep-alluvial Northern Plains (Bhabar,
Terai, Bhangar, Khadar) feeding the nation; the ancient tilted
Peninsular Plateau split by the Narmada into Central Highlands and
Deccan, rimmed by the Western and Eastern Ghats; the rain-starved
Thar beyond the Aravallis; the contrasting narrow-western and
deltaic-eastern coastal plains; and the tectonic Andaman–Nicobar
versus coral Lakshadweep islands.
[/summary]
